      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, I'd be happy to help! Religious conservatives often argue that physician-assisted dying contradicts their faith-based beliefs, particularly concerning the sanctity of life. They typically draw on scriptural teachings that life should be preserved and cared for until natural death, emphasizing the ethical responsibility to provide palliative care over hastened death. In contrast, religious liberals may also emphasize respect for life but focus more on issues of compassion, dignity, and personal autonomy. They argue that individuals should have the right to choose how and when to end their suffering, using the same moral language that they apply to issues like abortion and end-of-life decisions. The core arguments from both moderates and religious liberals revolve around balancing respect for life, compassion, and the individual’s right to make choices about their own bodies. Religious conservatives tend to emphasize these latter two to a lesser extent compared to their primary focus on preserving life at all costs.",
